## 1. System Overview and Mathematical Foundation

Tesla InductaCharge is a cable-free, ground-to-vehicle wireless charging system governed by a unified formal verification framework. The system is modelled as a switched Differential-Algebraic-PDE (DAE-PDE) hybrid system coupling three physical domains simultaneously: electromagnetic power transfer between ground pad and vehicle receiver, electrical conversion through a SiC Dual Active Bridge, and thermal dissipation through the semiconductor packaging layers.

The complete continuous state vector is:

**ξ = [λ₁,...,λₙ, q_in, q_out, T_j1,...,T_jM]ᵀ**

where λₖ = Lₖ·i_Lk are transformer leakage flux linkages, q_in and q_out are input and output capacitor charges, and T_ji are nodal junction temperatures across M semiconductor dies within the Under-Vehicle Charging Group (UVCG).

The governing equations in each discrete switching mode σ are:

**E_σ·ẋ = A_σ(T)·x + B_σ·u + w** — electrical swDAE, index ≤ 1

**C_th·Ṫ = −G_th(T)·T + B_th·p_loss(x,T) + D_th·T_amb** — thermal ODE

The coupling between domains is bidirectional: electrical losses p_loss(x,T) drive the thermal equation as heat sources, while junction temperatures feed back into A_σ(T) through temperature-dependent SiC parameters — on-resistance R_ds(on)(Tj) rising 38–55% from 25°C to 175°C, and switching energies E_on/off(Tj, I_ds).

