## 6. CPL Instability — Negative Incremental Impedance

When the UVCG delivers power to the Tesla battery pack, the battery management system draws constant power regardless of voltage — it behaves as a Constant Power Load (CPL). The output capacitor voltage dynamics are:

**C·v̇_o = i_L − P/v_o + d(t)** ... (3.1)

**L·i̇_L = u − v_o** ... (3.2)

where P > 0 is the true unknown battery charging power, u is the phase-shift derived control input, and d(t) is a bounded disturbance satisfying |d(t)| ≤ d̄ representing coil coupling variation, grid fluctuation, and measurement noise.

The incremental impedance of the CPL term P/v_o is:

**d(P/v_o)/dv_o = −P/v_o² < 0**

This negative incremental resistance makes the equilibrium of any passive linear regulator unstable. Linearising around the operating point (v_o*, i_L*), the characteristic equation has a root with positive real part whenever:

**P·L > C·v_o*⁴·R_eq**

This condition is satisfied precisely in the high-power fast-charging regime — large P, small R_eq — making CPL stabilisation the central control challenge.

