## 7. Adaptive Backstepping Controller

Define the three error coordinates:

**z₁(t) = v_o(t) − v_ref(t)** — voltage tracking error

**z₂(t) = i_L(t) − α(t)** — inductor current tracking error

**P̃(t) = P̂(t) − P** — power estimation error

where v_ref is a twice-differentiable bounded reference and α is the virtual control.

**Step 1 — Virtual control for voltage loop:**

Treating i_L as virtual control in (3.1) and substituting the power estimate P̂:

**α(t) = P̂(t)/v_o(t) − c₁·C·z₁(t) + C·v̇_ref(t)**

Substituting i_L = z₂ + α into (3.1) and using the definition of α:

**C·ż₁ = z₂ − c₁·C·z₁ + P̃/v_o + d** ... (3.7)

The damping term −c₁·C·z₁ provides exponential decay. The remaining terms z₂, P̃/v_o, d will be handled by the current loop and the adaptation law.

**Step 2 — Actual control for current loop:**

Differentiating z₂ and using (3.2):

**L·ż₂ = (u − v_o) − L·α̇**

Choose the control law:

**u = v_o + L·(α̇ − z₁/C − c₂·z₂)**

Substituting:

**L·ż₂ = −z₁ − c₂·L·z₂** ... (3.9)

The term −z₁ is deliberately introduced — it will cancel the cross-term z₁z₂ that appears in the Lyapunov derivative.

**Adaptation Law:**

**P̂̇ = −Γ·z₁/v_o, Γ > 0** ... (3.10)

This law drives the estimate in the direction that decreases the Lyapunov function. Since P is constant, P̃˙ = P̂̇.

