## 15. Conclusion

InductaCharge establishes wireless EV charging on a rigorous mathematical foundation. The port-Hamiltonian structure guarantees structural passivity and excludes converter-originated instability. The adaptive backstepping controller with formally proved ISS property eliminates CPL-induced instability across the full 10–350 kW operating range. The M-matrix thermal network with monotonicity guarantee enables the barrier certificate to provide a mathematically proved junction temperature invariant. The Lean 4 mechanised proofs eliminate the possibility of human algebraic error in the critical stability and safety arguments. The contract-based compositional framework ties all layers together into a certifiable safety case aligned with ISO 26262 ASIL-D and IEC 61508 SIL-3.

Appendix A — Detailed DAE Index Analysis

We analyze the switched Differential-Algebraic Equation (DAE) subsystem:

E_\sigma \dot{x} = A_\sigma(T)x + B_\sigma u + w

with state vector x = [\lambda_1, \dots, \lambda_N, q_{in}, q_{out}]^\top comprising transformer leakage flux linkages and capacitor charges. The switching mode \sigma encodes the on/off state of every SiC MOSFET in the Dual Active Bridge (DAB). The descriptor matrix E_\sigma is diagonal with entries L_k for inductive branches and 1 for capacitive nodes, but may be singular when ideal switches create algebraic constraints.

A.1 Network Topology in Each Mode

We consider an N-phase DAB with full-bridge primary and secondary. In any valid mode (no shoot-through), each phase leg connects either to the DC link or forms an open circuit. Replacing each ideal switch with:

· on‑state: a small resistance R_{ds(on)}
· off‑state: an open circuit (infinite resistance)

yields an RLC network with at least one energy‑storage element (inductor or capacitor) in every fundamental loop and at every fundamental cutset. This is a direct consequence of:

1. Dead‑time enforcement: Complementary switches never close simultaneously, preventing pure voltage‑source loops.
2. Positive leakage inductances: L_k > 0 for all k ensures that no loop consists only of voltage sources and closed switches.
3. Positive capacitances: C_{in}, C_{out} > 0 ensures that no cutset consists only of current sources and open switches.

A.2 Topological Index Theorem

Theorem (Estévez‑Schwarz & Tischendorf, 2000). For an electrical network whose graph contains at least one capacitor or inductor in every loop and every cutset after switch substitution, the resulting DAE has tractability index at most 1.

Index‑1 means that the algebraic constraints can be solved uniquely for the algebraic variables once the differential variables are known, and consistent initial conditions exist after any switching event. Mathematically, the matrix pencil \{E_\sigma, A_\sigma\} is regular and all local differentiation indices are ≤ 1.

Proof of Lemma 1. By construction, conditions 1–3 guarantee the topological hypothesis of the theorem. Hence in every \sigma, the DAE is index‑1. Consequently, the flow is well‑posed: for any initial condition satisfying the algebraic constraints, there exists a unique continuous solution. \square

The critical point for the wireless system is that the coupling coefficient k_c appears only in the mutual inductance M that enters the system matrix A_\sigma through the transformer coupling terms. Variation in k_c does not alter the network topology or the conditions for index‑1; it merely scales inductive coupling coefficients. Thus the index bound is robust under parking misalignment.

