Appendix B — Port‑Hamiltonian Model Construction

We derive the explicit matrices J(\sigma), R(\sigma), and G(\sigma) for a single‑phase DAB in a given switching mode, then generalize to N-phase.

B.1 Single‑Phase DAB

The lossless power stage (ignoring parasitics) has state x = [\lambda, q_{in}, q_{out}]^\top with Hamiltonian:

H(x) = \frac{\lambda^2}{2L} + \frac{q_{in}^2}{2C_{in}} + \frac{q_{out}^2}{2C_{out}}.

The gradient is \nabla H = [\lambda/L, \, q_{in}/C_{in}, \, q_{out}/C_{out}]^\top = [i_L, v_{in}, v_{out}]^\top.

In any mode, the dynamics are linear and can be written as:

\dot{x} = \mathcal{J}(\sigma) \nabla H + \mathcal{B}(\sigma) u

where \mathcal{J} is a skew‑symmetric matrix representing the interconnection structure (ideal transformer and switch routing). With losses, we have:

\dot{x} = [\mathcal{J}(\sigma) - \mathcal{R}(\sigma)] \nabla H + \mathcal{B}(\sigma) u,

where \mathcal{R}(\sigma) \succeq 0 is diagonal with entries R_{ds(on)} or winding resistance appropriately placed.

The standard pH form is  \dot{x} = [J(\sigma) - R(\sigma)]\nabla H + G(\sigma)u  with J = -J^\top, R = R^\top \succeq 0. Here G(\sigma) maps the phase‑shift input voltage.

Because the control input is the phase shift between bridges, it enters as a modulation of the effective voltage applied to the leakage inductance. In the averaged model, u is the phase‑shift itself; in the switched model, the input is a square‑wave voltage. For the pH representation of the switched system, one can treat the primary‑side DC voltage V_{in} as an input and the switching pattern as part of the time‑varying J(\sigma), G(\sigma). This is standard: the pH framework handles switched systems by allowing J, R, G to depend on the discrete state.

B.2 Skew‑Symmetry and Passivity Proof

For any \sigma:

\dot{H} = (\nabla H)^\top \dot{x} = (\nabla H)^\top [J(\sigma) - R(\sigma)] \nabla H + (\nabla H)^\top G(\sigma) u.

Since J is skew‑symmetric, (\nabla H)^\top J \nabla H = 0. Because R \succeq 0, -(\nabla H)^\top R \nabla H \leq 0. Thus

\dot{H} \leq y^\top u, \quad \text{with } y = G(\sigma)^\top \nabla H.

This is the dissipation inequality, establishing passivity of the DAB power stage. The inequality holds for every switching mode and therefore for any switching trajectory by concatenation of flows.

