Appendix C — Generalized Averaging Model (GAM)

C.1 Fourier Coefficient Dynamics

For a state variable x(t) with switching period T_s, the sliding Fourier coefficient of harmonic order k is:

\langle x \rangle_k (t) = \frac{1}{T_s} \int_{t-T_s}^{t} x(\tau) e^{-j k \omega_s \tau} d\tau.

The time derivative obeys:

\frac{d}{dt} \langle x \rangle_k = \langle \dot{x} \rangle_k - j k \omega_s \langle x \rangle_k.

We apply this to the DAB state x = [\lambda, q_{in}, q_{out}]^\top with the switched dynamics \dot{x} = f_\sigma(x,u). The averaged model retains only DC (k=0) and first harmonic (k=\pm 1). Because the transformer current has zero DC component in steady state, \langle i_L \rangle_0 \equiv 0, but its first harmonic is the main power transfer variable.

C.2 Phasor Equations for N‑Phase DAB

For each phase i, define the complex phasor for leakage flux linkage \Lambda_i = \langle \lambda_i \rangle_1 (or the corresponding current phasor I_i = \Lambda_i / L_i). The coupled coil voltage on the vehicle side is:

v_{coil,i} = j \omega_s M_i(\text{misalignment}) \, I_{p,i}

where M_i is the mutual inductance between the ground pad phase i and the receiver coil. Misalignment introduces variations in M_i.

The GAM dynamics for the N‑phase DAB with phase-shift inputs \phi_i take the linear parameter‑varying form:

\dot{z} = A(\phi) z + B\, v_{coil},
\qquad
v_{bat} = C z,

where z stacks the real and imaginary parts of all transformer current phasors and the DC capacitor voltages:

z = [\Re(I_1), \Im(I_1), \dots, \Re(I_N), \Im(I_N), v_{in}, v_{out}]^\top \in \mathbb{R}^{2N+2}.

The matrix A(\phi) encodes the DAB transformer dynamics, phase‑shift modulation, and capacitor charging. B maps the induced coil voltages (which depend on M) into the derivative of the current phasors, and C extracts the output voltage.

The explicit form of A(\phi) is derived by averaging the switched state equations; it is a standard result in DAB literature. The important property is that A(\phi) is affine in the trigonometric functions of \phi_i, which enables the adaptive controller design.

