Appendix D — Thermal Network Derivation and M‑Matrix Proof

D.1 Cauer RC Ladder Discretization

The 1D heat equation along the primary heat‑flow path (SiC die → substrate → baseplate → heatsink) is:

\rho c_p \frac{\partial T}{\partial t} = \frac{\partial}{\partial z} \left( k(T) \frac{\partial T}{\partial z} \right) + q_{loss}(t).

Using finite volume discretization with \ell layers, we define nodal temperatures T_i at layer interfaces and integrate over each control volume. The resulting ODE is:

C_{th,i} \dot{T}_i = \frac{T_{i-1} - T_i}{R_{i-1,i}} - \frac{T_i - T_{i+1}}{R_{i,i+1}} + Q_i,

where R_{i,i+1} = \Delta z_i / (k_i A_i) is the thermal resistance of layer i, C_{th,i} = \rho_i c_{p,i} A_i \Delta z_i the thermal capacitance, and Q_i the power dissipated in that node.

Collecting all nodes yields the matrix form:

C_{th} \dot{T} = -G_{th} T + B_{th} P_{loss} + D_{th} T_{amb},

with G_{th} having the tridiagonal structure:

G_{th} = 
\begin{bmatrix}
\frac{1}{R_{0,1}} & -\frac{1}{R_{0,1}} & 0 & \cdots \\
-\frac{1}{R_{0,1}} & \frac{1}{R_{0,1}} + \frac{1}{R_{1,2}} & -\frac{1}{R_{1,2}} & \\
0 & -\frac{1}{R_{1,2}} & \ddots & \\
\vdots & & &
\end{bmatrix}.

D.2 M‑Matrix Property

Definition. A matrix A \in \mathbb{R}^{n \times n} is an M‑matrix if A = sI - B with B \geq 0 (element‑wise) and s > \rho(B).

A sufficient condition is that A has positive diagonal and is strictly diagonally dominant with non‑positive off‑diagonals.

Proof of Lemma 2:

· Every diagonal entry G_{ii} is the sum of at least two positive conductances (to layers above and below, except at boundaries).
· Every off‑diagonal G_{i,i+1} = -1/R_{i,i+1} < 0, and all others zero.
· Thus the matrix has non‑positive off‑diagonals and positive diagonal.
· Because the network is connected (no isolated nodes) and each layer has a path to ambient, the matrix is irreducibly diagonally dominant. More simply, check strict diagonal dominance:

|G_{ii}| = \sum_{j \neq i} |G_{ij}| + \text{ (boundary terms)} > \sum_{j \neq i} |G_{ij}|

since at boundaries an extra term (e.g., to ambient) appears. Hence strict diagonal dominance holds.

· It is a standard result (Berman & Plemmons) that such a matrix is a non‑singular M‑matrix. Consequently, G_{th}^{-1} \geq 0 element‑wise.

D.3 Monotonicity Proof (Lemma 3)

The solution to \dot{T} = -C_{th}^{-1} G_{th} T + C_{th}^{-1} B_{th} P_{loss} with initial condition T(0) is:

T(t) = e^{-C_{th}^{-1} G_{th} t} T(0) + \int_0^t e^{-C_{th}^{-1} G_{th} (t-\tau)} C_{th}^{-1} B_{th} P_{loss}(\tau) d\tau.

Because G_{th} is an M‑matrix, C_{th}^{-1} G_{th} is also an M‑matrix (since C_{th} is diagonal positive, similarity transformation). The matrix exponential of an M‑matrix has all non‑negative entries: e^{-Mt} \geq 0 for t \geq 0 when M is an M‑matrix. Also C_{th}^{-1} B_{th} \geq 0. Therefore the entire integral kernel is non‑negative.

Now, if P_{loss}^1(t) \geq P_{loss}^2(t) component‑wise and T^1(0) \geq T^2(0), then

T^1(t) - T^2(t) = e^{-C_{th}^{-1} G_{th} t} (T^1(0)-T^2(0)) + \int_0^t e^{-C_{th}^{-1} G_{th} (t-\tau)} C_{th}^{-1} B_{th} (P_{loss}^1 - P_{loss}^2) d\tau \geq 0.

Thus T^1(t) \geq T^2(t) for all t. This monotonicity is the foundation of conservative temperature bounding using worst‑case loss estimates.

