Appendix E — Full ISS Lyapunov Proof for Adaptive Backstepping

We restate the system and provide the complete step‑by‑step derivation with all constants.

E.1 System Model

C \dot{v}_o = i_L - \frac{P}{v_o} + d(t), \qquad L \dot{i}_L = u - v_o,

with unknown constant P > 0, bounded disturbance |d(t)| \le \bar{d}. The reference v_{ref}(t) is smooth with bounded first two derivatives.

E.2 Error Dynamics and Control Law

Error variables: z_1 = v_o - v_{ref}, z_2 = i_L - \alpha, \tilde{P} = \hat{P} - P.

Virtual control \alpha is chosen to stabilize the z_1 dynamics if i_L were the actual control:

\alpha = \frac{\hat{P}}{v_o} - c_1 C z_1 + C \dot{v}_{ref}.

Substituting i_L = z_2 + \alpha into the voltage equation:

\begin{aligned}
C \dot{z}_1 &= z_2 + \alpha - \frac{P}{v_o} + d - C \dot{v}_{ref} \\
&= z_2 + \frac{\hat{P}}{v_o} - c_1 C z_1 + C \dot{v}_{ref} - \frac{P}{v_o} + d - C \dot{v}_{ref} \\
&= z_2 - c_1 C z_1 + \frac{\tilde{P}}{v_o} + d.
\end{aligned}

Thus,

\dot{z}_1 = -c_1 z_1 + \frac{1}{C} z_2 + \frac{1}{C} \frac{\tilde{P}}{v_o} + \frac{d}{C}. \tag{E.1}

Differentiating z_2:

\begin{aligned}
\dot{z}_2 &= \dot{i}_L - \dot{\alpha} = \frac{u - v_o}{L} - \dot{\alpha}.
\end{aligned}

The derivative \dot{\alpha} is computed using the chain rule:

\dot{\alpha} = \frac{\dot{\hat{P}} v_o - \hat{P} \dot{v}_o}{v_o^2} - c_1 C \dot{z}_1 + C \ddot{v}_{ref}.

We may expand \dot{v}_o = \dot{z}_1 + \dot{v}_{ref}. This yields a lengthy expression, but the backstepping design exploits the fact that we can assign the control u to cancel all known terms and introduce stabilizing damping.

Choose the actual control:

u = v_o + L \left( \dot{\alpha} - \frac{z_1}{C} - c_2 z_2 \right). \tag{E.2}

Then

