Appendix H — Phase‑Shedding Hybrid Automaton Details

H.1 State Definitions

The discrete state q_k \in \{1,\dots, N\} indicates the number of active DAB sub‑modules. The continuous state includes the adaptive controller errors, the power estimate, and the M junction temperatures of the semiconductor dies (one per active module).

H.2 Flow Conditions

In location q_k, the electrical dynamics are still governed by the same adaptive controller, but the per‑phase current is higher: the effective inductance and capacitance parameters scale with k. The thermal ODE for the j-th junction of an active module is:

\dot{T}_{j} = -\frac{1}{R_{th} C_{th}} (T_j - T_{amb}) + \frac{\beta_k}{C_{th}} (z_1^2 + z_2^2),

where \beta_k \propto 1/k (approximate scaling). Because fewer phases share the load, the conduction losses per module increase.

H.3 Hysteresis Guard Condition

To avoid chattering, we set thresholds T_{high} and T_{low} with T_{high} < T_{max} - \delta - \tau_{switch} \cdot \sup \dot{T}_j. The automaton transitions:

· from q_k to q_{k+1} when any junction temperature exceeds T_{high},
· from q_k to q_{k-1} when all junction temperatures fall below T_{low} and k>1.

The hysteresis band \Delta T = T_{high} - T_{low} must be larger than the worst‑case temperature rise during the finite phase‑activation delay \tau_{switch}. This ensures the safety margin \delta remains uncompromised.

H.4 Safety Proof Integration

The barrier certificate is established for each location independently, using the worst‑case loss coefficient \beta_{max}. Because monotonicity holds, if the temperature under maximal loss remains safe, then it is safe in all modes. The hybrid invariant is then proved by an inductive argument over discrete transitions, using the fact that the temperature is continuous at switches and the guard condition guarantees that immediately after a switch, the temperature is still strictly below the barrier.

